<h2 id="agents">Ask an AI agent to do it</h2>
<p>In a browser with <a href="/agents/">WebMCP</a>, tell your agent: <em>"Evaluate my repository against JRCv1, identify where it falls short, and help me make it Truly Free."</em> It can call the same tools this page uses:</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>assess_repository</code></td><td>Each pillar's status, concerns and next step</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>prepare_adoption_roadmap</code></td><td>Ordered steps with effort, impact and dependencies</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>generate_jrc_manifest</code></td><td>A candidate <code>jrc.json</code>, checked by the jrc engine</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>prepare_contribution</code></td><td>A PR description, checklist, patch or challenge log, plus links</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>run_challenge_checks</code></td><td>The challenge steps that are safe to run from public files</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>generate_assessment_report</code></td><td>The full report as Markdown or JSON</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>All of them are read-only. An agent can <em>prepare</em> a pull request, an issue or a challenge submission. It can't open one, because no tool can sign in to GitHub or write to any repository. You review what it prepared and submit it yourself.</p>

<h2 id="cli">Or use the command line</h2>
<pre><code>jrc assess https://github.com/OWNER/REPO --out jrc-adopt   # public GitHub repository
jrc assess .                                               # a local checkout, no network
jrc assess --snapshot jrc-snapshot.json                    # reproduce a saved assessment</code></pre>
<p><code>jrc assess</code> writes the same files this page offers. <a href="/check/">Getting the tool</a> takes one command.</p>

<h2 id="raise-the-flag">Raise the Flag</h2>
<p>No application, fee, login or approval. Publish your evidence, say how far you've got, and invite people to check.</p>
<ol>
  <li><a href="/check/">Write a <code>jrc.json</code></a> for your project (the candidate above is a start) and run <code>jrc check</code>.</li>
  <li>Publish the report (<code>jrc report --out jrc-report</code>) in your repository.</li>
  <li>Optionally show the badge, linked to that report.</li>
  <li>Ask someone unaffiliated to <a href="/challenge/">take the challenge</a> on your project.</li>
</ol>

<h3>What raising the flag means</h3>
<ul>
  <li>You've written down, in a checkable format, how your project meets each pillar, and what it doesn't meet yet.</li>
  <li>The evidence files you cite exist, and any you pinned by hash haven't changed.</li>
  <li>You're willing to be corrected in public.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What it does not mean</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>It doesn't certify or endorse anything.</strong> Nobody, including this project, has checked that your statements are true. The badge says <em>self-reported</em> for that reason.</li>
  <li><strong>It isn't a licence.</strong> Your project's rights come from its actual licence. JRCv1 doesn't grant or require any particular one, though the code-and-rights pillar asks you to document yours.</li>
  <li><strong>It isn't independent verification.</strong> That only exists when a named, unaffiliated reviewer publishes their own reproduction report, and even then <code>jrc</code> records the claim without verifying it.</li>
  <li><strong>It isn't permanent.</strong> A report describes one manifest at one date. Re-run it when things change.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Three honest levels</h3>
<div class="table-scroll"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">What you can claim</th><th scope="col">What it is not</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Exploring</strong></td><td>You're interested and you've written down your gaps.</td><td>Any conformance claim at all</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Self-reported</strong></td><td>Your manifest passes <code>jrc check</code> and cites evidence for the pillars you list.</td><td>Independent certification</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Independently reproduced</strong></td><td>An unaffiliated reviewer has reproduced your claim and published their method and results.</td><td>A permanent badge. Claims are tied to a version and a date.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Partial adoption is normal and useful. "3 of 5 applicable pillars evidenced, help wanted on the rest" is a good thing to publish.</p>

<h3>Names and the flag</h3>
<p>"Jolly Roger Charter", "JRCv1" and the flag aren't registered trademarks, and you don't need permission to use them to describe an honest self-assessment. Please don't use them to suggest this project has reviewed or endorsed yours. The artwork is CC0.</p>

<h3>Projects flying the flag</h3>
<p><span class="badge">Listed: 0</span> We only list projects that ask to be listed and publish a report. Listing a project doesn't mean we've audited it.</p>

